<h2>How to spot a case-study-worthy client</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Measurable before/after.</strong> Real numbers: PageSpeed went from X to Y, organic traffic moved from A to B, leads per month grew from N to M.</li>
  <li><strong>Defined business outcome.</strong> "Booked $40K in new commercial work in the first 90 days" is the shape that lands. Vague "happy with the site" is not.</li>
  <li><strong>Client comfortable being named.</strong> Anonymous case studies work but rank lower in trust. Named is preferred; offer anonymous as fallback.</li>
  <li><strong>Vertical relevance.</strong> Aim for one case study per trade I serve (HVAC, roofing, plumbing, electrical, inspection, landscaping) over the next 18 months.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Permissions form</h2>
<p>Before any case-study work begins, the client signs a one-page release. Single Google Form / Web3Forms intake covers:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Permission to use the business name and logo.</li>
  <li>Permission to publish specific numbers (PageSpeed, traffic, lead volume, revenue ranges &mdash; not exact dollars unless explicitly granted).</li>
  <li>Permission to use a quote attributed to the owner.</li>
  <li>Permission to publish before/after screenshots of the old and new site.</li>
  <li>Right of review: client gets to see the case study and request changes before publication.</li>
  <li>Right to revoke: client can ask for the study to be unpublished any time, no questions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Interview script (45-60 minutes)</h2>
<ol>
  <li>What was the business situation before we started? (the old site, the lead volume, the specific frustration)</li>
  <li>What pushed you to actually hire someone? (the trigger event)</li>
  <li>Why me, specifically? (the differentiator that closed the deal)</li>
  <li>What was the working relationship like during the build? (the client experience)</li>
  <li>What changed after launch? (the measurable outcome)</li>
  <li>What surprised you? (the second-order effect they did not expect)</li>
  <li>What would you tell another business owner considering this? (the testimonial-shaped close)</li>
</ol>
<p>Record the call (with consent). Transcribe (Whisper or similar). Draft the case study from the transcript &mdash; the client's actual words are 10&times; better than my paraphrase.</p>

<h2>Case-study structure</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Headline:</strong> the outcome in one line. "How [Client] doubled mobile leads in 90 days."</li>
  <li><strong>Above-the-fold metrics block:</strong> 3-4 numbers, big and bold.</li>
  <li><strong>Background.</strong> Who is the client, what do they do, what was the situation.</li>
  <li><strong>The work.</strong> What I built. Specific. With before/after screenshots.</li>
  <li><strong>The numbers.</strong> Real metrics. Time-bound. Ideally cross-referenced to the analytics dashboard.</li>
  <li><strong>The quote.</strong> One client quote that anchors the whole piece.</li>
  <li><strong>The context.</strong> What this means for other businesses in similar situations.</li>
  <li><strong>CTA.</strong> Free audit or discovery call.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Publishing cadence</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One full case study per quarter.</li>
  <li>One mini-update per quarter (a launched-site post on the blog with one screenshot, one paragraph, one number) for the case studies that do not warrant a full write-up.</li>
  <li>Refresh the metrics on existing case studies once per year.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PR / earned-media targets</h2>
<p>Trade publications are higher-leverage than general business press for a niche service. Pitch list:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Roofers Coffee Shop</strong> (roofing trade media)</li>
  <li><strong>Home Inspector Magazine, ASHI Reporter, ICA's e-newsletter</strong> (inspection)</li>
  <li><strong>ACHR News, Contracting Business</strong> (HVAC)</li>
  <li><strong>Plumbing &amp; Mechanical, PHC News</strong> (plumbing)</li>
  <li><strong>EC&amp;M, Electrical Contractor</strong> (electrical)</li>
  <li><strong>Landscape Management, Lawn &amp; Landscape</strong> (landscaping)</li>
  <li><strong>Local business journals</strong> (Denver Business Journal, etc.) for the chamber-adjacent stories.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PR pitch structure</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Subject line names the outcome ("How a Boulder landscaper doubled spring-install bookings with one site change").</li>
  <li>1-paragraph hook with the angle for THIS publication's readers.</li>
  <li>1-paragraph case-study summary.</li>
  <li>Offer: a specific quote from the client + access to the analytics dashboard for verification.</li>
  <li>One-line bio + one link.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common issues</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Numbers are not clean enough to publish.</strong> Define the measurement window upfront (90 days post-launch is the standard). Use the same window across all case studies for comparability.</li>
  <li><strong>Client gets cold feet on numbers.</strong> Offer ranges or percentage growth instead of absolute dollars. Always honor the request.</li>
  <li><strong>Pitch goes nowhere.</strong> Most pitches do not land. The ratio is roughly 1 placement per 5-10 thoughtful pitches. Keep going.</li>
</ul>

